 "thesis": "Method-test thesis: Harsha Engineers is the largest independent maker of precision bearing cages (steel, brass, polyamide; 20-2,000 mm), selling mainly to a handful of global bearing majors from Ahmedabad (Changodar, Moraiya, Bhayla), Changshu (China) and Ghimbav (Romania), plus bushings for wind gearboxes, stampings and a solar-EPC side business (~11% of revenue, ~5% of EBITDA). India Engineering earns ~22-24% EBITDA margins; the group earns ~11.5% ROIC because of Romania losses, a working-capital-heavy model (~135 days) and a Rs ~600 cr capex wave (Advantek Bhayla phases 1-2, China phase 2) that is still ramping. The 5-year outcome depends on whether that capex earns well above its recent ~11% incremental return.",
 "variant_view": "Market: at Rs 438 the stock trades at 24.4x trailing P/E and 14.3x EV/EBITDA - below its short listed history (FY23-26 FY-end median 28.8x) and well below bearing makers (43-50x), near the closest chain peer Rolex Rings (22.1x). Analysts on the Q2 FY27 call asked about Romania downsizing, the conservative H2 guidance, metal pass-through and Advantek breakeven; nobody asked about group ROIC or the utilisation of the capacity being built. Our read: the worksheet's reverse DCF finds no growth path at the historical ~11-14% ROIIC that justifies the price - it needs a year-10 NOPAT margin of ~24.5% (vs 9.0% now) on the base growth path, i.e. the market is already paying for India-Engineering-style returns on the new capital. Evidence against that: Advantek runs at ~54% utilisation (Reg-30 28-May-2026) yet is adding 12 m pieces (Rs 110 cr) and 'Rs 350 cr plus' in total; the Rs 117 cr/yr bushing contract (Aug-2025) is 'a little bit delayed'; Romania has missed operating breakeven targets since FY25; FY26 CFO was Rs 68 cr on Rs 278 cr EBITDA. Evidence for: India Engineering +23% in H1 FY27 with 23.7% EBITDA margin, outsourcing projects worth Rs 75 cr + Rs 75-100 cr in the pipeline, a strategic sourcing deal with a European major, net cash ~Rs 95 cr, CARE AA- (Mar-2024), promoter 75% unpledged. No claimable mispricing: we have no information edge on customer outsourcing decisions; the question we can track (utilisation and ROIC on the new capex) is dated and public. Departure from the outside view: raw P(blow-up) 5% is below the class 15% (strong balance sheet, AA- rating, no pledge) - anchored at the default w=0.5, which raises it; we do not claim enough evidence to go below 0.5. Checked by paper calls thesis:paper-call:HARSHA:*.",
